WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.899 align:start position:0%
 
Oh  the world is a noisy place every day  were surrounded by rush-hour traffic  obnoxious neighbors countless planes  zipping by overhead it seems impossible  to get any peace and quiet in the modern  world in one of my previous videos we  learned about the loudest sound ever  made but what if you want it to be as  removed from sound as you could possibly  be believe it or not there are still  some places on earth that are so quiet  all you'll be able to hear is your own  ears ringing in this video we're going  to look at five of the absolute quietest  places on the planet the first stop on  our silent journey is the HOH rainforest  part of Olympic National Park in  Washington this massive force consists  of nine hundred and twenty-two thousand  acres of unspoiled natural beauty with  the smallest number of roads in the  continental US the whole valley is home  to what's called one square inch of  silence this area marked by a small red  stone is part of a project to encourage  people to listen to their surroundings  while this is supposedly the quietest  natural place in the country you'll  still hear the natural ambience of the  breeze leaves rustling and forest  animals according to one commercial  pilot many airlines have voluntarily  rerouted flights to avoid creating noise  pollution at the square inch of silence  for those of us who just want a break  from the sounds of cars planes and other  man-made distractions Olympic National  Park is the perfect getaway not quiet  enough let's take a look at the crow  Natsuki nature reserve in Russia this  remote fiercely breathtaking landscape  is known as the land of Fire and Ice not  because of any relation to Game of  Thrones but because of its many geysers  and volcanoes the reserve is vast with  trees few and far between and the chilly  climate keeps animal noise to a minimum  though you might encounter the  occasional bear or to crow Natsuki  reserve was created in 1934 and its  current boundary contains an area of  10,000 990 square kilometers or 4240  square miles its main purpose is as an  area for the study of natural sciences  the variety of flora and fauna as well  as mountain ranges and both active and  extinct volcanoes make the reserve the  perfect place to study everything from  biology to chemistry to geology and  everything in between the area is so far  removed from civilization you'll never  see or hear a motor vehicle including  your own that's because the reserve is  so protected you have to sign up with  one of the few approved companies to  shuttle you in on a helicopter and  that's if you're one of the lucky 3,000  people allowed to go per year  if you do manage to get past all the red  tape to visit the reserve be prepared to  pay a fee equivalent to about 700 US  dollars for a one-day trip the crew  Natsuki nature reserve is the ideal spot  for people who appreciate the idea that  statistically speaking even if all three  thousand yearly visitors showed up on  the same day they'd still be separated  from well over 99 percent of the rest of  the people on earth  if the icy climate isn't your idea of  relaxing head over to the Negev desert  in Israel far from the bustling cities  of Jerusalem and Tel Aviv this 13,000  square kilometer expanse of dust and  sand makes up over 55 percent of the  country's land area if you trek further  away from the road into the desert you  can hear your own ears ring from the  silence all you'll hear out there is the  occasional breeze and the dirt crumbling  underfoot for some the silence of the  desert is the most strikingly spiritual  experience of the Holy Land  despite the deserts mostly harsh  landscape there are still a select few  plant and animal species that thrive  there if you're patient and more than a  little lucky you might catch a glimpse  of the endangered Arabian leopard or the  critically endangered negative tortoise  the desert is definitely in stark  contrast to the crow Natsuki reserve  with average temperatures in the warm  season often exceeding 38 degrees  Celsius or 100 degrees Fahrenheit so if  you're planning a trip to the Negev  desert be sure to go in the middle of  summer to avoid as many people as  possible and get a truly silent  experience just remember to bring  sunscreen if the crow not ski reserve is  too cold and the desert is too hot  consider taka a cenote in Tulum Mexico  if you thought it was quiet above-ground  wait till you experience this  subterranean cave not only is this cave  entirely devoid of sound but it's also  completely dark if you're brave turn out  your headlamp and swim out into the  pitch-black water of the cavern for the  ultimate sensory deprivation experience  all you'll be able to hear is your own  breathing and the occasional drop of  water from the stalactites just keep in  mind that while short-term sessions of  sensory deprivation can be very relaxing  and conducive to meditation extended  periods of sensory deprivation can  result in extreme anxiety hallucinations  bizarre thoughts and depression so if  being totally alone and depressed in a  subterranean cave doesn't sound like  your idea of a good time consider one of  the other destinations on our list and  for the final stop on our trip a  man-made Marvel called the anechoic  chamber located in Minnesota according  to the Guinness Book of World Records  the chamber is 90  9.9% sound absorbent running at the  title of the absolute quietest place on  earth you enter the strange room through  large heavy vault style doors the  chamber is constructed of a double layer  of steel and foot thick concrete covered  in crosshatch sound absorbent buffers  and even the floor is suspended by mesh  to avoid any sound from footsteps once  the heavy doors closed behind you the  noise level in the room drops to  negative nine decibels by comparison a  library is usually around 30 decibels  Steven or filled the lab founder says he  challenges people to sit in the room in  the dark and see how long they can last  he says when it's quiet ears will adapt  the quieter of the room the more things  you'll hear you'll hear your heart  beating sometimes you can hear your  lungs hear your stomach gurgling loudly  in the anechoic chamber you become the  sound the longest anyone has lasted in  the room is 45 minutes of course the  room isn't just for unsettling its  visitors when it's not being used as a  torture chamber it serves as a test room  for manufacturers to gauge the volume of  their components it's a fascinating room  and quite the feat of sonic engineering  but not being able to enjoy the silence  for more than a few minutes kind of  defeats the purpose of our hypothetical  trip still for anyone interested in how  the human body sounds in absolute  silence the anechoic chamber is a must  visit so maybe there's such a thing as  too quiet but for anyone who wants the  ultimate noise for you get away you've  got your shortlist these places are the  absolute quietest on earth if you'd like  to learn more about the places on this  list follow the links in the description  remember to follow second thought on  your favorite social media and subscribe  to keep up to date with the latest  content thanks for watching and I'll see  you in the next video

